 row }i\\text{ is }(i,\\sigma(i)).\n\\]\n\nThus the set of uncovered squares is the permutation matrix \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); all other \\(n^{2}-n\\) squares must be covered by tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Geometry of a tile\n\nConsider a tile whose set of rows is an interval \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and whose set of columns is an interval \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIf there exist \\(i\\in I\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\min J\\le \\sigma(i)\\le \\max J,\n\\]\n\nthen the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) would belong to the tile, contradicting the rules.  \nHence for every tile either  \n\n\\[\n\\max J<\\min_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)\\qquad\\text{(the tile lies completely to the left of all holes in its rows)},\n\\]\n\nor  \n\n\\[\n\\min J>\\max_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)\\qquad\\text{(the tile lies completely to the right of all holes in its rows)}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently each tile is of one of the following two forms:\n\n* **left\u2011justified:** \\(J=[1,c]\\) with \\(c<\\min_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)\\);\n* **right\u2011justified:** \\(J=[d,n]\\) with \\(d>\\max_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound for the number of left\u2011justified tiles\n\nFix a column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nA_j=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)>j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nA left\u2011justified tile that contains column \\(j\\) must contain **all** rows of a maximal consecutive block of \\(A_j\\); otherwise the tile would have to skip a row where column \\(j\\) is present, which is impossible because a tile is a single rectangle.  \nThus each maximal block of consecutive rows inside \\(A_j\\) forces a distinct left\u2011justified tile.\n\nLet \\(c_j\\) be the number of such blocks. Then the total number \\(L\\) of left\u2011justified tiles satisfies  \n\n\\[\nL\\ge\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}c_j .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTo evaluate the sum, write \\(b_i^{\\,j}=1\\) if \\(\\sigma(i)>j\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise.  \nA block of \\(A_j\\) starts at row \\(i\\) exactly when \\(b_i^{\\,j}=1\\) and either \\(i=1\\) or \\(b_{i-1}^{\\,j}=0\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nc_j=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} b_i^{\\,j}\\bigl(1-b_{i-1}^{\\,j}\\bigr),\\qquad b_{0}^{\\,j}=0 .\n\\]\n\nSumming over \\(j\\) and interchanging the order of summation gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}c_j\n&=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} b_i^{\\,j}\\bigl(1-b_{i-1}^{\\,j}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=(\\sigma(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\n   \\mathbf 1_{\\{\\sigma(i)>j\\ge\\sigma(i-1)\\}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\ge2\\) the inner indicator is true precisely for the integers  \n\n\\[\nj=\\sigma(i-1),\\sigma(i-1)+1,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\n\\]\n\nwhen \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(i-1)\\); otherwise it is never true.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}c_j\n=(\\sigma(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the number of right\u2011justified tiles\n\nThe argument is symmetric.  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nB_j=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(d_j\\) be the number of maximal consecutive blocks of \\(B_j\\).  \nEvery right\u2011justified tile that contains column \\(j\\) must contain an entire block, so the number \\(R\\) of right\u2011justified tiles satisfies  \n\n\\[\nR\\ge\\sum_{j=2}^{n}d_j .\n\\]\n\nExactly the same counting as in the previous section yields  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=2}^{n}d_j\n=\\bigl(n-\\sigma(1)\\bigr)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,\\sigma(i-1)-\\sigma(i)\\bigr). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Total number of tiles\n\nAdding (6) and (7) we obtain a universal lower bound for any permutation \\(\\sigma\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL+R &\\ge (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\n      \\bigl|\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)\\bigr|\\\\\n    &= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\n      \\bigl|\\sigma(i+1)-\\sigma(i)\\bigr| . \\tag{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the entries of a permutation are distinct, for every \\(i\\)\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\sigma(i+1)-\\sigma(i)\\bigr|\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nL+R\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling attaining the bound\n\nTake the monotone permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\sigma(i+1)-\\sigma(i)|=1\\) for all \\(i\\), so (8) gives exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\nConstruct the tiles as follows.\n\n*For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)* place a vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_k^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. column \\(k\\) together with all rows strictly **below** the empty square \\((k,k)\\).\n\n*For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\)* place a horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_k^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. column \\(k\\) together with all rows strictly **above** the empty square \\((k,k)\\).\n\nThe \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each lies entirely on one side of the diagonal, and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) (the required uncovered squares). Hence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion for the original problem\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nThus the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
