"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Encoding the uncovered squares.**  \n   Let \\(n=2025\\).  \n   In each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) there is exactly one uncovered unit square; denote its column by \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \n   The map \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation, and the set of uncovered squares is the permutation matrix  \n   \\[\n   U=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Structure of a tile.**  \n   A tile is a rectangle whose rows form an interval \\(I\\) and whose columns form an interval \\(J\\).  \n   Because no uncovered square may belong to a tile, for every \\(i\\in I\\) we must have  \n   \\[\n   \\sigma(i)\\notin J .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Hence for a fixed tile either  \n\n   * **left\u2011justified:** \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,c\\}\\) with \\(c<\\min_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)\\), or  \n   * **right\u2011justified:** \\(J\\subseteq\\{d,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(d>\\max_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)\\).\n\n   (If a tile contained rows whose uncovered squares lie on opposite sides of \\(J\\), then \\(J\\) would have to contain a column equal to some \\(\\sigma(i)\\), contradicting (1).)\n\n3. **Counting left\u2011justified tiles.**  \n   For each column \\(j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-1)\\) define  \n   \\[\n   A_j=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)>j\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n   In a left\u2011justified tile that uses column \\(j\\) the whole set of its rows must be a **maximal** consecutive block of \\(A_j\\); otherwise the tile would have to skip a row of \\(A_j\\) and would then contain the uncovered square of that row.  \n   Let \\(c_j\\) be the number of maximal blocks of \\(A_j\\).  \n   If \\(L\\) denotes the total number of left\u2011justified tiles, then  \n\n   \\[\n   L\\ge\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}c_j .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   A direct counting of the blocks gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}c_j\n   =(\\sigma(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)\\bigr).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Counting right\u2011justified tiles.**  \n   Symmetrically, for each column \\(j\\;(2\\le j\\le n)\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   B_j=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\,\\},\n   \\]\n   let \\(d_j\\) be the number of maximal blocks of \\(B_j\\), and let \\(R\\) be the number of right\u2011justified tiles. Then  \n\n   \\[\n   R\\ge\\sum_{j=2}^{n}d_j\n   =\\bigl(n-\\sigma(1)\\bigr)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,\\sigma(i-1)-\\sigma(i)\\bigr).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Universal lower bound.**  \n   Adding (3) and (4) we obtain, for every permutation \\(\\sigma\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L+R &\\ge (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\n          \\bigl|\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)\\bigr|   \\\\\n        &= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\n          \\bigl|\\sigma(i+1)-\\sigma(i)\\bigr|.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the entries of a permutation are distinct, \\(|\\sigma(i+1)-\\sigma(i)|\\ge1\\) for every \\(i\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   L+R\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **any** admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n6. **Achieving the bound.**  \n   Choose the monotone permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the identity).  \n   Then each difference \\(|\\sigma(i+1)-\\sigma(i)|\\) equals \\(1\\), so (5) gives exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n   An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles is:\n\n   *For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)* place a vertical rectangle  \n   \\[\n   V_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. column \\(k\\) together with all rows strictly **below** the empty square \\((k,k)\\).\n\n   *For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\)* place a horizontal rectangle  \n   \\[\n   H_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. column \\(k\\) together with all rows strictly **above** the empty square \\((k,k)\\).\n\n   The \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each is a legitimate tile, and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), which are precisely the required uncovered squares. Hence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n7. **Conclusion for the original problem.**  \n   With \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the answer is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nIn each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) there is exactly one unit square that is not covered; denote its column by \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.  \nThe set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other \\(n^{2}-n\\) squares must be covered by tiles, each tile being a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines and whose interior contains no point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Geometry of a single tile  \n\nConsider a tile \\(T\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nI=\\{\\,\\text{rows occupied by }T\\,\\},\\qquad\nJ=\\{\\,\\text{columns occupied by }T\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of integers, say  \n\n\\[\nI=[a,b],\\qquad J=[c,d]\\qquad (a\\le b,\\;c\\le d).\n\\]\n\nBecause no uncovered square may belong to \\(T\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)\\notin J\\quad\\text{for every }i\\in I. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence for each \\(i\\in I\\) either \\(\\sigma(i)<c\\) or \\(\\sigma(i)>d\\).\n\nIf there exist \\(i_{1},i_{2}\\in I\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_{1})<c\\) and \\(\\sigma(i_{2})>d\\), then the interval \\([c,d]\\) lies strictly between \\(\\sigma(i_{1})\\) and \\(\\sigma(i_{2})\\).  \nSince the rows of a rectangle are contiguous, the whole interval \\(I\\) would contain a row \\(i_{0}\\) whose uncovered column lies between \\(\\sigma(i_{1})\\) and \\(\\sigma(i_{2})\\); that column would belong to \\(J\\), contradicting (7).  \nTherefore **all** rows of a tile lie on the same side of its column interval. Consequently each tile is of one of the two types:\n\n* **left\u2011justified:** \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,c\\}\\) with \\(c<\\min_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)\\);\n* **right\u2011justified:** \\(J\\subseteq\\{d,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(d>\\max_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for left\u2011justified tiles  \n\nFix a column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) and define  \n\n\\[\nA_j=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)>j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a left\u2011justified tile contains column \\(j\\), then every row of that tile belongs to \\(A_j\\); otherwise the tile would contain an uncovered square on its right side, violating the left\u2011justified condition.  \nMoreover, because the rows of a tile form a single interval, a left\u2011justified tile can cover **only one maximal consecutive block** of \\(A_j\\).  \n\nLet \\(c_j\\) be the number of maximal consecutive blocks of \\(A_j\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) denotes the total number of left\u2011justified tiles, then\n\n\\[\nL\\ge\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}c_j .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTo evaluate the sum, write \\(b_i^{\\,j}=1\\) if \\(\\sigma(i)>j\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise.  \nA block of \\(A_j\\) starts at row \\(i\\) precisely when \\(b_i^{\\,j}=1\\) and either \\(i=1\\) or \\(b_{i-1}^{\\,j}=0\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nc_j=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} b_i^{\\,j}\\bigl(1-b_{i-1}^{\\,j}\\bigr),\\qquad b_{0}^{\\,j}=0 .\n\\]\n\nSumming over \\(j\\) and interchanging the order of summation yields  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}c_j\n&=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} b_i^{\\,j}\\bigl(1-b_{i-1}^{\\,j}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=(\\sigma(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\n   \\mathbf 1_{\\{\\sigma(i)>j\\ge\\sigma(i-1)\\}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\ge2\\) the inner indicator is true exactly for the integers  \n\n\\[\nj=\\sigma(i-1),\\sigma(i-1)+1,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\n\\]\n\nwhen \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(i-1)\\); otherwise it is never true.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}c_j\n=(\\sigma(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)\\bigr).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8) and (9) we obtain the lower bound for left\u2011justified tiles:\n\n\\[\nL\\ge (\\sigma(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)\\bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for right\u2011justified tiles  \n\nThe argument is symmetric.  \nFor each column \\(j\\;(2\\le j\\le n)\\) set  \n\n\\[\nB_j=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nlet \\(d_j\\) be the number of maximal consecutive blocks of \\(B_j\\), and let \\(R\\) be the number of right\u2011justified tiles.  \nExactly as before we obtain  \n\n\\[\nR\\ge\\sum_{j=2}^{n}d_j\n=\\bigl(n-\\sigma(1)\\bigr)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,\\sigma(i-1)-\\sigma(i)\\bigr).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Universal lower bound  \n\nAdding (10) and (11) gives, for every permutation \\(\\sigma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL+R &\\ge (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\n      \\bigl|\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)\\bigr|  \\\\\n    &= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\n      \\bigl|\\sigma(i+1)-\\sigma(i)\\bigr|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause the entries of a permutation are distinct, \\(|\\sigma(i+1)-\\sigma(i)|\\ge 1\\) for every \\(i\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nL+R\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** admissible tiling of the \\(n\\times n\\) board uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the monotone permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the identity).  \nThen \\(|\\sigma(i+1)-\\sigma(i)|=1\\) for all \\(i\\), and (12) yields exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\nDefine the following rectangles:\n\n*For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)*\n\\[\nV_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\na vertical rectangle of width\u202f1 covering column \\(k\\) from row \\(k+1\\) down to row \\(n\\).\n\n*For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\)*\n\\[\nH_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n\\]\na horizontal rectangle of width\u202f1 covering column \\(k\\) from row\u202f1 up to row \\(k-1\\).\n\nThe \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each is aligned with the grid, and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\)).  \nThese diagonal squares are precisely the uncovered squares required by the problem, because each row \\(i\\) and each column \\(i\\) contains exactly the square \\((i,i)\\) that is left empty.\n\nHence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion for the original board  \n\nFor the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
