 =\\{(j,j)\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\ni.e. the holes become the main diagonal.  \nSince a relabelling of rows and columns does not affect the number of tiles\nrequired, we may (and shall) assume from now on that\n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the following two rectangles:\n\n* **Vertical rectangle**\n  \\[\n  V_i=\\{(r,i)\\mid r=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n  \\]\n  It occupies the part of column \\(i\\) that lies strictly below the diagonal\n  hole \\((i,i)\\).\n\n* **Horizontal rectangle**\n  \\[\n  H_i=\\{(i,c)\\mid c=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n  \\]\n  It occupies the part of row \\(i\\) that lies strictly to the right of the\n  diagonal hole \\((i,i)\\).\n\nThe rectangles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, as are\n\\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\); moreover a vertical rectangle never meets a horizontal\none, because a vertical rectangle uses only columns \\(\\le n-1\\) while a\nhorizontal rectangle uses only rows \\(\\le n-1\\).\n\nEvery unit square that is not a hole belongs either to some \\(V_i\\) (if it is\nbelow the diagonal) or to some \\(H_i\\) (if it is above the diagonal).  Hence\nthe family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},\\;H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers exactly the complement of \\(H\\).  The number of tiles in \\(\\mathcal T\\)\nis\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus a placement with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles for any permutation\n\nWe now prove that **any** admissible tiling must contain at least \\(2n-2\\)\ntiles, irrespective of the permutation \\(\\pi\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,\\pi(i)+1), & \\text{if }\\pi(i)<n,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{undefined}, & \\text{if }\\pi(i)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(r_i\\) is the square immediately to the **right** of the hole in row\n\\(i\\) (when such a square exists).  \nSimilarly, for each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n(\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,j), & \\text{if }\\pi^{-1}(j)<n,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{undefined}, & \\text{if }\\pi^{-1}(j)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(c_j\\) is the square immediately **below** the hole in column \\(j\\)\n(when it exists).\n\nExactly two of the \\(2n\\) squares \\(\\{r_i\\}\\cup\\{c_j\\}\\) are missing:\nthe square to the right of the hole in the last column and the square below\nthe hole in the last row.  Consequently we obtain a set\n\n\\[\nS=\\{r_i\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\}\\cup\\{c_j\\mid \\pi^{-1}(j)<n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontaining precisely \\(2n-2\\) distinct unit squares.\n\n**Claim.** No two squares of \\(S\\) can be covered by the same rectangle that\ncontains no hole.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake two distinct squares \\(x,y\\in S\\). Their rows are distinct and their\ncolumns are distinct, because each row contributes at most one element of the\nform \\(r_i\\) and each column contributes at most one element of the form\n\\(c_j\\).  Let the rectangle \\(R\\) be the smallest axis‑parallel rectangle\ncontaining both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).  The opposite corners of \\(R\\) are the rows\nand columns of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\); consequently the intersection of the row of\nthe left‑hand square with the column of the right‑hand square is a cell of the\nform \\((k,k)\\), i.e. a hole of the permutation.  Hence any rectangle that\ncontains both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) must also contain a hole, which is forbidden.\n∎\n\nSince each tile can contain at most one element of \\(S\\), any admissible\ntiling needs at least \\(|S|=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Minimality\n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles (Section 2) and have\nproved that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles (Section 3).  Therefore\nthe minimum possible number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient."
