mediately **below** the hole in column \\(j\\)\n(when it exists).\n\nExactly two of the \\(2n\\) squares \\(\\{r_i\\}\\cup\\{c_j\\}\\) are missing:\nthe square to the right of the hole in the last column and the square below\nthe hole in the last row.  Consequently we obtain a set\n\n\\[\nS=\\{r_i\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\}\\cup\\{c_j\\mid \\pi^{-1}(j)<n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontaining precisely \\(2n-2\\) distinct unit squares.\n\n---\n\n**Claim.** No two squares of \\(S\\) can belong to the same rectangle that\ncontains no hole.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider two distinct squares \\(x,y\\in S\\).\n\n*If both are right\u2011neighbors* (say \\(x=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\) and \\(y=(k,\\pi(k)+1)\\)),\ntheir rows are distinct.  Assume \\(i<k\\).  \nIf \\(\\pi(i)<\\pi(k)\\) then the hole \\((k,\\pi(k))\\) lies inside the rectangle\nspanned by rows \\(i,\\dots ,k\\) and columns \\(\\pi(i)+1,\\dots ,\\pi(k)+1\\);\nif \\(\\pi(i)>\\pi(k)\\) the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) lies inside that rectangle.\nHence any rectangle containing both right\u2011neighbors would also contain a hole.\n\n*If both are below\u2011neighbors* the argument is symmetric, using the rows\ninstead of the columns.\n\n*If one is a right\u2011neighbor* \\((i,\\pi(i)+1)\\) and the other a below\u2011neighbor\n\\((\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,j)\\), then the rectangle determined by their rows and\ncolumns necessarily contains the cell \\((i,j)\\).  Since \\((i,j)\\) is a hole\n(because the hole in row \\(i\\) is at column \\(\\pi(i)=j\\)), the rectangle would\ncontain a hole.\n\nThus in every case a rectangle that contains two distinct squares of \\(S\\)\nmust also contain a hole, which is forbidden.  Consequently each square of\n\\(S\\) must lie in a different tile. \u220e\n\nBecause \\(|S|=2n-2\\), any admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality\n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles (Section\u202f2) and have\nproved that no tiling can use fewer tiles (Section\u202f3).  Therefore the minimum\npossible number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient."
